class Solution {
  public int bookingRackEvenKeyCensus(int[][] drawers) {
    int[][] a=drawers; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[][]rows=a.clone();Arrays.sort(rows,Comparator.comparingInt((int[]r)->r[1]).thenComparingInt(r->r[0]));int end=Integer.MIN_VALUE;for(int[]r:rows)if(r[0]>=end){out.add(r[1]-r[0]);end=r[1];}}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x%2==0)answer++;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build acceptedLengths

Each booking is [start, end]. It uses time from start up to, but not including, end. Sort bookings by end time, from earliest to latest. For matching end times, put the earlier start first. Accept the first booking. Then accept a booking only if it starts at or after the end of the last one accepted. Record the length, end - start, of each accepted booking. Keep acceptance order. Negative times are allowed. With no bookings, `acceptedLengths` is empty.

Return the answer

Count the even numbers in `acceptedLengths`. Store the count in `evenCount`. A number is even when it divides by 2 with no remainder. Complete `bookingRackEvenKeyCensus` so it returns `evenCount`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `acceptedLengths` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

